@@ -8,6 +8,8 @@ Browser and editor share Overview, Search, Indexes, Activity, Agents, Service an
 
 Bundled MiniLM retrieval and optional Arbiter answers have separate roles. The service installer includes MiniLM; optional Focus/Context weights are acquired separately through explicit provisioning when a verified public source is available. Arbiter is off by default and requires explicit immutable-index enrollment and a verified supported profile. Its implementation is not a claim of independent model, device, quality or platform qualification. `airkctl arbiter status` and actual tool schemas describe served capabilities. Product Help has one generated source catalog for the editor, browser and static site and remains available offline where bundled.
 
+Arbiter research credits include [CoSQA (Huang et al., 2021)](https://aclanthology.org/2021.acl-long.442/), [CodeSearchNet (Husain et al., 2019)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.09436), ContextBench, [TechQA (Castelli et al., 2020)](https://aclanthology.org/2020.acl-main.117/), QASPER, HotpotQA and Requests contributors. The [Arbiter README](docs/arbiter/README.md) records full authors, separate code/data terms and historical versus candidate use; [research notices](third_party/research/ARBITER-RESEARCH-NOTICES.md) preserve original attribution in the installer legal bundle. No raw research corpus is bundled, and acknowledgement of a candidate dataset does not assert training admission or qualified models.
+
 **Agents need no token (U110, 15 November 2026).** Coding agents connect to the service's `https://127.0.0.1:<port>/mcp` endpoint with no credential; any `Authorization` header is ignored, and any local process that can reach 127.0.0.1 can read every index. TLS issuer trust is still required. `airkctl agents connect --client claude|codex|vscode` (or Explorer **Agents** → **Connect**) writes only the endpoint URL and `X-AIRK-Response-Mode`; `airkctl agents repair` strips tokens and credential helpers from older entries. Each read names its scope (`index_ids`, `index_set_ids` or `workspace`); `list_scopes` lists indexes, index sets and the workspace defaults that an Explorer window's applied search scope stores (`airkctl scope default|defaults`). See [agent setup](docs/agent-integration.md). In the current source, `airkctl --output json agents manual --client codex --project "<absolute project folder>"` returns the exact target file, format and complete entry; replace `codex` with `claude` or `vscode` for those clients. Merge that entry into the named file while preserving other settings. Automatic editing can refuse a folder whose complete file and recovery copy cannot be protected. The source UI exposes the same entry under **Set up manually**; those UI changes still need sealed-asset and installed checks. Regenerate the entry after a service port change. Older notices below that mention agent tokens, credential helpers or open connections are historical.
 
